{
  "case_id": "2009-08-1322",
  "document_hash": "3e7c092447d7bcc7749abe61fbb8a715dce0d53925efb7ca841f2d7f39213ca4",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1322",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e7c092447d7bcc7749abe61fbb8a715dce0d53925efb7ca841f2d7f39213ca4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e7c092447d7-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:03 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1322",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e7c092447d7bcc7749abe61fbb8a715dce0d53925efb7ca841f2d7f39213ca4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e7c092447d7-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "EAJA provides that in an appropriate case a court shall award to a prevailing party fees and other expenses incurred by that party."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1322",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e7c092447d7bcc7749abe61fbb8a715dce0d53925efb7ca841f2d7f39213ca4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e7c092447d7-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Isn't it true that the Commissioner of Social Security in the past paid EAJA awards directly to attorneys and that this is still done in cases in which the client has assigned the payment to an attorney?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1322",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e7c092447d7bcc7749abe61fbb8a715dce0d53925efb7ca841f2d7f39213ca4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e7c092447d7-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: What authority does a court have to direct that the money be paid to the attorney?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1322",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e7c092447d7bcc7749abe61fbb8a715dce0d53925efb7ca841f2d7f39213ca4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e7c092447d7-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What would happen in the case -- in case it is a prisoner who is this prevailing party? Would the -- would the attorney fee be paid to the prisoner? And if not, to whom would it be paid?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Yang",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1322",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e7c092447d7bcc7749abe61fbb8a715dce0d53925efb7ca841f2d7f39213ca4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e7c092447d7-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "EAJA applies not only in the Social Security context; it applies in all civil actions in which -- not sounding in tort, in which there\u2019s not another specific fee provision, brought -- brought by or against the United States."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1322",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e7c092447d7bcc7749abe61fbb8a715dce0d53925efb7ca841f2d7f39213ca4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e7c092447d7-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: And does that mean --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Yang",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1322",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e7c092447d7bcc7749abe61fbb8a715dce0d53925efb7ca841f2d7f39213ca4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e7c092447d7-0001-0002",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The IRS is of the view that attorney fee awards to prevailing parties, whether it's EAJA or otherwise, are deemed to be taxable income to the client."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Leach",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1322",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e7c092447d7bcc7749abe61fbb8a715dce0d53925efb7ca841f2d7f39213ca4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e7c092447d7-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I\u2019d like to discuss with you this morning four reasons why Catherine Ratliff, and not the government, is entitled to receive the fee for the legal services she performed, that Congress invited her to perform, to show that the government's position was legally erroneous and was not even substantially justified."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1322",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e7c092447d7bcc7749abe61fbb8a715dce0d53925efb7ca841f2d7f39213ca4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e7c092447d7-0001-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF JAMES D. LEACH ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1322",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e7c092447d7bcc7749abe61fbb8a715dce0d53925efb7ca841f2d7f39213ca4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e7c092447d7-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Well, let me -- can trusts -- can trusts recover EAJA fees?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1322",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "3e7c092447d7bcc7749abe61fbb8a715dce0d53925efb7ca841f2d7f39213ca4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e7c092447d7-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So what does it mean? Well, the government\nconcedes at page 8 of its reply brief that that language\n\"incurred by\" a prevailing party is supported by the\nclient's implied or express obligation to pay the\nattorney the fee received. So this is where the\ngovernment's argument is circular, I think,\nJustice Alito, in the sense that that \"incurred by\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1322",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "3e7c092447d7bcc7749abe61fbb8a715dce0d53925efb7ca841f2d7f39213ca4",
